bridgerton the show that stole 16 hours  of my life that i actually enjoyed for  those of you who don't know what  bridgeton is it's an alternate  historical drama series that follows the  bridgerton family as they search for  love during the season in high london  society when my wife asked me to watch  this show with her i thought fine i'll  watch it but i'll probably play on my  phone or not pay much attention but boy  was i wrong i was fully engaged for two  eight hour plus long seasons bridgette  has a peculiar way of keeping you  entertained while not really providing  much substance it is as i would call it  a mesmerizing mess  [Music]  before we get into it obviously there  will be spoilers for the show so if  you're worried about it being spoiled  watch the show first then come back to  this video okay so first of all i don't  intend to talk about the plot of the  show that much in this video instead  i'll be talking about themes and how the  series is able to pull people in and not  let them go until they've watched every  single episode but how exactly does the  show keep people so captivated the three  main reasons audiences are so mesmerized  by bridgerton are character investment  tension and high stakes each season  introduces us to a new set of characters  with the bridgertons the featheringtons  and the duke of hastings being  introduced in the first season and the  sharma family being introduced in the  second season we're also introduced to a  mystery character named lady whistled  down who with the help of her almost  daily gossip pamphlet has about as much  social power as the queen of england  herself the writers for the show do an  excellent job of getting the audience  invested in each of the characters  struggles and desires in a way that i  haven't seen much before in television  shows it's because of this investment  that we quickly start to care what  happens to the bridge or tens of the  featheringtons and the sharmas and the  over-the-top drama that ensues between  them for example we care a lot when  daphne finds out simon can have children  but that he just doesn't want any we  care when lady whistle down's identity  is almost revealed and we care so much  when we come to understand why anthony  and kate just can't be together to  someone who hasn't gotten invested into  these characters and their stories these  things would probably seem insignificant  but to those who have been pulled into  the show's drama you can't help but feel  empathy for the characters the show is  also quite good at creating tension by  only giving the audience certain pieces  of information and not the entire story  the tension begins to rise in very tense  situations we don't know what's gonna  happen we simply hope for the best  outcome but expect the worst for example  when anthony and kate come close to  kissing multiple times when we know they  absolutely should not part of us still  hopes that they do because we know the  love and passion they share for each  other these scenes in particular do a  great job of building tension across  episodes until they finally all come  together the underlying reason we get  such tense scenes though is because the  writers have already set such high  stakes for the characters having the  story set in a partially historical  1800s england the stakes are very high  for the young lords and ladies of london  especially during this season it's well  established that a gentleman and a lady  must not ever be alone together without  a chaperone since they could be doing  something that might taint the lady's  honor it's also established that all  ladies must find a husband or else  they'll turn into an old spinster with  no one to take care of them lastly it's  established that all gentlemen must find  a wife so that they can do their duty in  furthering their family's line by having  a son because the scene is set with such  stringent rules and expectations the  stakes are very high for all of our main  characters for example anytime two  characters of opposite sexes are in a  room alone you're always afraid someone  will burst into the room and find them  together right before demanding that  they get married because they were alone  together also you understand just how  important to the characters it is to  find a spouse not just for love but for  all the previously mentioned reasons  when even a single member of one of the  high-class london families doesn't  follow every single social rule they're  shunned from society so the stakes are  at their highest during the season in  which both seasons of bridgerton take  place now that we understand exactly why  bridgeton is so mesmerizing let's dive  into the mess of a show we received and  i don't necessarily mean mess in a bad  way instead i would liken it to a high  production quality much better version  of a soap opera set in 1800s england it  actually has very good dialogue and  writing likely due to the fact that it's  based on a book series of the same name  and many people know that series based  on books with good writing can often  have great results until they run out of  source material and start to crash and  burn like game of thrones anyways there  were several things about the show that  i didn't really like first of all the  way the characters talk can be a little  infuriating at times like whenever they  say mamas and papas i cannot stand it  also i was pretty let down when we found  out that lady whistledown was not  actually julie andrews but penelope i  mean come on i would love to see mary  poppins in this show also penelope was  probably one of my least favorite  characters so making her the secret  writer that's been narrating the entire  season was a huge letdown one thing i  actually really liked about the show  though was its use of classical music  covers of modern pop songs the show's  director and executive producer julianne  robinson said the music covers were  inspired by the use of classic rock  songs in the 2001 heath ledger film a  knight's tale hearing classical covers  of thank you next bad guy and even  wildest dreams was a bit surreal but i  enjoyed it a lot i think bridgerton will  likely become a guilty pleasure of mine  in the future since i'll probably have  to re-watch it when season 3 comes out  to those who haven't already seen  bridgerton i don't know if i would  necessarily recommend watching it if you  have an extra 16 hours and it sounds  interesting enough then get ready for a  completely unexpected roller coaster of  emotions that you won't be able to turn  away from although the show was a bit of  a mess i certainly think it will  continue to grow in popularity  mesmerizing audiences around the world  anyways that's gonna be all for today if  you like what you saw maybe check out  the video on your screen for some  different content i make videos on  basically anything that interests me in  the world of entertainment also don't  forget to like and subscribe as it truly  does help me out and thanks for watching  rocket riley  you
 

